1. job of each class, relations between them, with accordance to the UML

Package ascii_art:
- Shell: The main class and entry point of the program. Manages the interactive command
  loop, reading user commands and delegating to the appropriate handler methods. Holds
  references to SubImgCharMatcher (for charset management), AsciiOutput (for rendering),
  and PaddedImage (for the loaded image). Orchestrates the full pipeline: image loading,
  padding, brightness computation, and algorithm execution.
- AsciiArtAlgorithm: Executes a single pass of the ASCII art conversion. Receives a
  pre-computed brightness grid (double[][]) and a SubImgCharMatcher, and maps each
  brightness value to the closest ASCII character. Supports a reverse mode that uses
  (1 - brightness) for inverted output. A new instance is created per run.
- KeyboardInput: A Singleton wrapper around java.util.Scanner. Provides a static
  readLine() method for reading user input. Uses lazy initialization via getObject().
- CharsetTooSmallException: Thrown when asciiArt is invoked with fewer than 2 characters.
- ExceedingBoundariesException: Thrown when a resolution change exceeds min/max bounds.
- InvalidCommandException: Thrown when the user enters an unrecognized command.
- InvalidFormatException: Thrown when a command argument has incorrect format.

Package ascii_output:
- AsciiOutput: An interface defining a single method out(char[][]) for outputting
  a 2D character grid. Shell holds a reference of this interface type.
- ConsoleAsciiOutput: Implements AsciiOutput. Prints characters to System.out, each
  followed by a space, with rows separated by newlines.
- HtmlAsciiOutput: Implements AsciiOutput. Writes an HTML file with styled monospace
  text. Handles HTML entity escaping for <, >, &. Adjusts font size based on grid width.

Package image:
- Image: Represents an image as a 2D Color[][] array. Can be loaded from a file
  (via ImageIO) or constructed from a pixel array. Provides getPixel(row, col),
  getWidth(), getHeight(). Used by PaddedImage, SubImageBrightness, and Shell.
- PaddedImage: Wraps an Image with symmetric white-pixel padding so that both
  dimensions become powers of two. Provides subImages(resolution) which splits the
  padded image into a grid of square Image tiles. Used by Shell for tile decomposition.
- SubImageBrightness: A stateless utility class. Computes the average grey-scale
  brightness of an Image using the luminance formula (0.2126*R + 0.7152*G + 0.0722*B),
  normalized to [0.0, 1.0]. Used by Shell when building the brightness grid.

Package image_char_matching:
- CharConverter: A supplied utility class that renders a character as a 16x16
  BufferedImage using Courier New font and converts it to a boolean[][] grid.
  Used by SubImgCharMatcher to compute raw character brightness.
- SubImgCharMatcher: The core matching engine. Maintains a configurable charset
  (TreeSet), caches raw brightness values (HashMap), and lazily computes normalized
  brightness via linear stretch. Provides getCharByImageBrightness(double) to find
  the closest character for a given brightness. Used by AsciiArtAlgorithm and Shell.

Relations:
Shell has-a SubImgCharMatcher, has-a AsciiOutput (interface), has-a PaddedImage.
Shell creates AsciiArtAlgorithm (passing brightness grid + matcher + reverse flag).
AsciiArtAlgorithm uses SubImgCharMatcher.
PaddedImage wraps Image and produces Image[][] tiles.
SubImgCharMatcher uses CharConverter (static calls).
Shell uses SubImageBrightness (static calls) on Image tiles from PaddedImage.
AsciiOutput is implemented by ConsoleAsciiOutput and HtmlAsciiOutput.

2. JAVA data structure used in the code, where in the code, and why- running time, and memory usage

TreeSet<Character> (SubImgCharMatcher, field "charset"):
Used to store the current character set. TreeSet was chosen because it maintains
characters in sorted ASCII order automatically, which is needed for the printCharSet()
method and for tie-breaking (lower ASCII wins). It provides O(log n) add and remove
operations and O(n) sorted iteration. Memory usage is O(n) where n is the charset size.

HashMap<Character, Double> (SubImgCharMatcher, field "rawBrightness"):
Used to cache the raw brightness of every character ever computed. HashMap was chosen
for O(1) amortized get/put operations. This acts as a persistent cache: when a character
is removed from the charset and later re-added, its brightness does not need to be
recomputed (the glyph rendering via CharConverter is expensive). Memory is O(k) where
k is the total number of distinct characters ever added across the lifetime.

HashMap<Character, Double> (SubImgCharMatcher, field "normalizedBrightness"):
Stores the linearly-stretched brightness for each character in the current charset.
Rebuilt lazily (only when dirty flag is set and getCharByImageBrightness is called).
HashMap gives O(1) lookup during the matching loop. Memory is O(n) where n is the
current charset size.

Color[][] (Image, field "pixelArray"):
A 2D primitive-wrapper array storing the pixel data of the image. Provides O(1)
random access by row and column. Memory is O(width * height).

double[][] (Shell, field "cachedBrightness"):
Caches the brightness grid for the current resolution. Avoids redundant brightness
computation when re-running asciiArt at the same resolution with a different charset.
Memory is O(rows * cols) where rows and cols depend on resolution.

Image[][] (returned by PaddedImage.subImages()):
A 2D array of sub-image tiles produced by splitting the padded image at a given
resolution. Each tile is a square Image. Memory is O(gridRows * gridCols), where each
tile itself holds O(tileSize^2) pixels.

boolean[][] (returned by CharConverter.convertToBoolArray()):
A 16x16 grid representing the rendered glyph of a character, where true = white pixel.
Used by SubImgCharMatcher.computeRawBrightness() to compute the fraction of filled
pixels. Memory is O(1) (fixed 16x16 = 256 booleans).

3. in the shell class we used switch-case to handle use commands, each command is
transferred to its own method to be handled. each method can throw 1 of 4 exceptions
we created - invalid command, invalid format, exceeding boundaries, or charset is
too small. then the shell catches the exceptions and print each its own message.

The run() method contains the main command loop with a switch-case on the first word
of user input. Each case delegates to a dedicated handler method:
- "exit" -> sets running = false (no handler method, no exception)
- "chars" -> matcher.printCharSet() (no exception)
- "add" / "remove" -> handleAddRemove(parts, true/false)
    Throws InvalidFormatException if the argument is missing, not a valid single
    character, not a valid range (e.g. "a-z"), and not "all" or "space".
- "res" -> handleRes(parts) -> changeResolution(boolean up)
    handleRes throws InvalidFormatException if the sub-command is not "up" or "down".
    changeResolution throws ExceedingBoundariesException if the new resolution
    (doubled or halved) would go above maxResolution or below minResolution.
- "reverse" -> toggles the reverse flag (no exception)
- "output" -> handleOutput(parts)
    Throws InvalidFormatException if the argument is not "console" or "html".
- "asciiArt" -> handleAsciiArt()
    Throws CharsetTooSmallException if the charset has fewer than 2 characters.
- default -> throws InvalidCommandException for any unrecognized command.

All four exception types are caught by a single multi-catch block in the run() loop:
catch (InvalidCommandException | InvalidFormatException |
       ExceedingBoundariesException | CharsetTooSmallException e)
The caught exception's message is printed via System.out.println(e.getMessage()),
and the loop continues so the user can retry.

Additionally, IOException is caught separately during image loading at the start of
run(). If the image file cannot be read, the method returns silently.

4. changes to SubImgCharMatcher API and why

We added two public methods to the SubImgCharMatcher API that were not in the original
supplied interface:

- getCharsetSize(): Returns the number of characters currently in the charset
  (charset.size()). This was added so that Shell.handleAsciiArt() can check whether
  the charset has at least 2 characters before running the algorithm, in order to
  throw CharsetTooSmallException. Without this method, Shell would need direct access
  to the internal TreeSet, breaking encapsulation.

- printCharSet(): Prints all characters in sorted ASCII order, each followed by a
  space, on a single line. This was added to support the "chars" command in Shell.
  The method iterates the TreeSet (which maintains sorted order) and prints to
  System.out. Placing this logic in SubImgCharMatcher keeps the charset printing
  logic close to the data structure that owns the charset, rather than exposing the
  internal collection to Shell.

We also changed the internal implementation for performance:
- Added a "normalizedDirty" boolean flag for lazy normalization. In the original
  implementation, recomputeNormalized() was called eagerly inside addChar() and
  removeChar() every time the charset changed. In the final version, addChar() and
  removeChar() only set the dirty flag, and recomputeNormalized() is called lazily
  on the next getCharByImageBrightness() call. This avoids redundant recomputation
  when multiple add/remove operations happen in sequence (e.g., "add all" adds 95
  characters but normalizes only once).
- The rawBrightness cache is no longer cleared on removeChar(). In the original
  version, removeChar() also removed the entry from rawBrightness, so re-adding
  the same character required re-rendering its glyph. In the final version, removed
  characters stay in rawBrightness, making re-addition O(1).
- The recomputeNormalized() method now iterates only over the current charset
  (not the full rawBrightness map) when finding min/max, ensuring correct
  normalization even when rawBrightness contains entries for removed characters.

5. changes in supplied code and why

The following files were supplied and were NOT modified:
- image/Image.java
- image_char_matching/CharConverter.java
- ascii_output/AsciiOutput.java
- ascii_output/ConsoleAsciiOutput.java
- ascii_output/HtmlAsciiOutput.java
- ascii_art/KeyboardInput.java

The following files were written by us (not supplied):
- image/PaddedImage.java - Pads images to power-of-two dimensions and splits into tiles.
- image/SubImageBrightness.java - Computes average brightness of sub-image tiles.
- image_char_matching/SubImgCharMatcher.java - Character-to-brightness matching engine.
- ascii_art/AsciiArtAlgorithm.java - The core algorithm mapping brightness to characters.
- ascii_art/Shell.java - The interactive command shell.
- ascii_art/CharsetTooSmallException.java - Custom exception for charset size validation.
- ascii_art/ExceedingBoundariesException.java - Custom exception for resolution bounds.
- ascii_art/InvalidCommandException.java - Custom exception for unknown commands.
- ascii_art/InvalidFormatException.java - Custom exception for malformed arguments.

No supplied code was modified.
